Cell[TextData[{
 "The distinction the report draws matters. A residual that lands in the \
stabilizer group is a correction that worked; one that lands in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["N", "TI"], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     StyleBox["S", "TI"], ")"}], "\\", 
    StyleBox["S", "TI"]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "187adc3d-3961-76c2-4819-d667b8145c28"],
 " is a ",
 StyleBox["silent logical error",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", the failure mode the whole subject exists to bound; and a syndrome the \
table cannot decode is a third thing again. On the bit-flip code, a Z error \
is invisible to every check:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"641c78eb-7f03-7846-0c24-71b57ab64aac"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"QECCode", "[", "\"\<BitFlipCode\>\"", "]"}], "[", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<CorrectionCycle\>\"", ",", " ", "\"\<ZII\>\""}], "]"}], 
  "[", "\"\<Outcome\>\"", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->
  "In[21]:= ",ExpressionUUID->"5bfcc75d-c21d-6fb0-0983-df06f7a8359a"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"LogicalError\"\>"], "Output",
 CellLabel->
  "Out[21]= ",ExpressionUUID->"112ae6ec-4b5c-6ad2-4729-ffb66ee93838"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Encoding circuits", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"6121b9de-dddd-aca8-10de-309bd469f6f2"],

Cell["\<\
A code is not useful until something prepares a codeword. The encoder follows \
Procedure 6.6 of Gottesman's 2026 book, with the gate-to-row-operation \
dictionary of its Table 6.1.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"30209679-023a-ee91-61df-0823b7ccb4fb"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The construction is worth understanding, because it is the one place where \
a linear-algebra fact turns directly into a circuit. Write the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["m", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "29bc1606-c22d-409d-5f43-8863f7d812e7"],
 " generators as the columns of a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"2", 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"], "\[Cross]", 
    StyleBox["m", "TI"]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7bf1e1bf-5b3e-c64b-298e-f8f95ec8ac91"],
 " binary matrix, the X parts stacked above the Z parts. Left-multiplying by \
a Clifford gate is then a ",
 StyleBox["row operation", "TI"],
 " on that matrix, and the dictionary is small and fixed: a Hadamard on qubit \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["q", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3119d855-3ffd-7401-6726-d21fb9885f6b"],
 " swaps the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["q", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "62a6d1b5-4e36-fa69-125d-c8ff6bc0c8b3"],
 "-th X row with the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["q", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7c41cdc9-5ab3-1ef5-247e-e4935f4664df"],
 "-th Z row; a phase gate adds the X row into the Z row; a CNOT adds one X \
row to another and one Z row back the other way; a controlled-Z crosses X \
into Z between two qubits. Relabelling which product of generators counts as \
a generator is a ",
 StyleBox["column", "TI"],
 " operation, and costs no gate at all."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"51fce014-dca1-0630-0783-fa51d5546d1a"],

Cell["\<\
So the problem becomes Gaussian elimination with two kinds of move, and the \
target is the matrix of the trivial code \[LongDash] every generator a single \
Z. Reduce to it, record the gates used, and the encoder is that sequence run \
backwards, since every gate in the dictionary is its own inverse except the \
phase gate, whose inverse is three of itself.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"657c1c65-980e-511d-1303-964f19bb2067"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "One step remains after inverting. Reducing and re-inflating gets the ",
 StyleBox["group", "TI"],
 " right but not necessarily the ",
 StyleBox["signs", "TI"],
 ": the prepared state is stabilized by each generator up to a minus. A Pauli \
correction fixes it, and finding it is again linear algebra \[LongDash] solve \
over GF(2) for the Pauli whose symplectic products with the generators match \
the pattern of wrong signs, then apply it."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0f15dbd5-5f35-596c-352a-d29f5ac02bb6"],

Cell["\<\
The book's own recipe for that fixup is to prepend an X on each qubit whose \
generator came out negative; the package instead solves the general system, \
which handles the completed generator set in one step. Both are correct and \
produce circuits of the same length, on every code tested.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7e9b7e66-629a-1114-26b9-7040172f607e"],

Cell["The encoding circuit of the five-qubit code:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"585a51e6-774f-2dd2-087a-48c002fa7738"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
